      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Village of Crystal Lakes officially formed in June 1986, marking the start of its municipal identity.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://cityofcrystallakes.org/history/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "By August 1996, Crystal Lakes transitioned from a village to a Fourth‑Class City, expanding its local governance.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://cityofcrystallakes.org/history/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Crystal Lakes spans about 1,020 acres and boasts five lakes, the largest—Crystal Lake—covering 83 acres.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17,
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://cityofcrystallakes.org/history/",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Construction of Crystal Lake’s dam began in 1969 on a former limestone quarry, creating the 90‑acre fishing spot.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://mdc.mo.gov/discover-nature/places/crystal-lake",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The Missouri Department of Conservation runs the lake’s fishery, offering anglers largemouth bass, bluegill, crappie, channel catfish, and flathead catfish.",
